```python
student = {"name": "Alice", "age": 20}

# Add new pair
student["major"] = "CS"
# student is now {"name": "Alice", "age": 20, "major": "CS"}

# Modify existing value
student["age"] = 21
# student is now {"name": "Alice", "age": 21, "major": "CS"}
```
Both adding a new pair and modifying an existing value use the same assignment syntax: `dictionary[key] = value`.

The `update()` method merges a dictionary (or an iterable of key-value pairs) into the current dictionary. If a key from the update source already exists in the target dictionary, its value is overwritten. If the key does not exist, it's added.

```python
d1 = {"a": 1, "b": 2}
d2 = {"b": 3, "c": 4}
d1.update(d2)
# d1 is now {"a": 1, "b": 3, "c": 4}
```
